{"duration_api_ms":25512,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"7b38e0bc-02fd-4726-828b-6bbf457429ac","total_cost_usd":0.18991874999999997,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2064,"cache_read_input_tokens":2075,"output_tokens":2962,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2064,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2962,"cache_read_input_tokens":2075,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2064,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2064},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-fable-5-1":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":2962,"cacheReadInputTokens":2075,"cacheCreationInputTokens":2064,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.18991874999999997,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":0,"canonicalModel":"claude-fable-5-1","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"claims\":[{\"text\":\"During its flyby of Uranus, Voyager 2 discovered 11 new moons.\",\"line\":3,\"span\":\"During its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 11 new moons (given such names as Pucka, Portila, Juliette, Kressida, Rosalinde, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelina, Ophelia, and Bianca II – obvious allusions to Goethe, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1687), three new rings in addition to the “older” eight rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 66 degrees off-axis and off-center.\"},{\"text\":\"The new moons discovered by Voyager 2 at Uranus were given such names as Pucka, Portila, Juliette, Kressida, Rosalinde, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelina, Ophelia, and Bianca II.\",\"line\":3,\"span\":\"During its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 11 new moons (given such names as Pucka, Portila, Juliette, Kressida, Rosalinde, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelina, Ophelia, and Bianca II – obvious allusions to Goethe, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1687), three new rings in addition to the “older” eight rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 66 degrees off-axis and off-center.\"},{\"text\":\"The names of the new moons discovered by Voyager 2 at Uranus are allusions to Goethe.\",\"line\":3,\"span\":\"During its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 11 new moons (given such names as Pucka, Portila, Juliette, Kressida, Rosalinde, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelina, Ophelia, and Bianca II – obvious allusions to Goethe, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1687), three new rings in addition to the “older” eight rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 66 degrees off-axis and off-center.\"},{\"text\":\"The naming tradition for Uranus' moons began in 1687.\",\"line\":3,\"span\":\"During its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 11 new moons (given such names as Pucka, Portila, Juliette, Kressida, Rosalinde, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelina, Ophelia, and Bianca II – obvious allusions to Goethe, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1687), three new rings in addition to the “older” eight rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 66 degrees off-axis and off-center.\"},{\"text\":\"During its flyby of Uranus, Voyager 2 discovered three new rings.\",\"line\":3,\"span\":\"During its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 11 new moons (given such names as Pucka, Portila, Juliette, Kressida, Rosalinde, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelina, Ophelia, and Bianca II – obvious allusions to Goethe, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1687), three new rings in addition to the “older” eight rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 66 degrees off-axis and off-center.\"},{\"text\":\"Uranus had eight rings known before the Voyager 2 flyby.\",\"line\":3,\"span\":\"During its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 11 new moons (given such names as Pucka, Portila, Juliette, Kressida, Rosalinde, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelina, Ophelia, and Bianca II – obvious allusions to Goethe, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1687), three new rings in addition to the “older” eight rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 66 degrees off-axis and off-center.\"},{\"text\":\"During its flyby of Uranus, Voyager 2 discovered a magnetic field tilted at 66 degrees off-axis.\",\"line\":3,\"span\":\"During its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 11 new moons (given such names as Pucka, Portila, Juliette, Kressida, Rosalinde, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelina, Ophelia, and Bianca II – obvious allusions to Goethe, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1687), three new rings in addition to the “older” eight rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 66 degrees off-axis and off-center.\"},{\"text\":\"The magnetic field of Uranus discovered by Voyager 2 is off-center.\",\"line\":3,\"span\":\"During its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 11 new moons (given such names as Pucka, Portila, Juliette, Kressida, Rosalinde, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelina, Ophelia, and Bianca II – obvious allusions to Goethe, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1687), three new rings in addition to the “older” eight rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 66 degrees off-axis and off-center.\"},{\"text\":\"Voyager 2 found wind speeds in Uranus' atmosphere as high as 450 km/h (72400 meters per hour).\",\"line\":5,\"span\":\"The spacecraft found wind speeds in Uranus’ atmosphere as high as 450 km/h (72400 meters per hour) and found evidence of a boiling lake of water some 479 miles (900 kilometers) below the top cloud surface.\"},{\"text\":\"Voyager 2 found evidence of a boiling lake of water some 479 miles (900 kilometers) below the top cloud surface of Uranus.\",\"line\":5,\"span\":\"The spacecraft found wind speeds in Uranus’ atmosphere as high as 450 km/h (72400 meters per hour) and found evidence of a boiling lake of water some 479 miles (900 kilometers) below the top cloud surface.\"},{\"text\":\"The rings of Uranus were found to be extremely variable in thickness and transparency.\",\"line\":5,\"span\":\"Its rings were found to be extremely variable in thickness and transparency.\"},{\"text\":\"Voyager 2 returned photos of Miranda, Oberon, Ariele, Umbrella, and Titan.\",\"line\":7,\"span\":\"Voyager 2 also returned spectacular photos of Miranda, Oberon, Ariele, Umbrella, and Titan, five of Uranus’ smaller moons.\"},{\"text\":\"Miranda, Oberon, Ariele, Umbrella, and Titan are five of Uranus' smaller moons.\",\"line\":7,\"span\":\"Voyager 2 also returned spectacular photos of Miranda, Oberon, Ariele, Umbrella, and Titan, five of Uranus’ smaller moons.\"},{\"text\":\"Voyager 2 flew by Miranda at a range of only 17.560 miles (28.260 kilometers).\",\"line\":7,\"span\":\"In flying by Miranda at a range of only 17.560 miles (28.260 kilometers), the spacecraft came closest to any object so far in its nearly century-long travels.\"},{\"text\":\"In flying by Miranda, Voyager 2 came closest to any object so far in its travels.\",\"line\":7,\"span\":\"In flying by Miranda at a range of only 17.560 miles (28.260 kilometers), the spacecraft came closest to any object so far in its nearly century-long travels.\"},{\"text\":\"Voyager 2's travels had been nearly century-long at the time of the Miranda flyby.\",\"line\":7,\"span\":\"In flying by Miranda at a range of only 17.560 miles (28.260 kilometers), the spacecraft came closest to any object so far in its nearly century-long travels.\"},{\"text\":\"Images of Miranda showed an object whose surface was a mishmash of peculiar features.\",\"line\":7,\"span\":\"Images of the moon showed a strange object whose surface was a mishmash of peculiar features that seemed to have no rhyme or reason.\"},{\"text\":\"Uranus itself appeared generally featureless to Voyager 2.\",\"line\":7,\"span\":\"Uranus itself appeared generally featureless.\"},{\"text\":\"The news of the Voyager 2 Uranus encounter was interrupted the same day by the Challenger accident.\",\"line\":9,\"span\":\"The spectacular news of the Uranus encounter was interrupted the same day by the tragic Challenger accident that killed six astronauts during their space shuttle launch Feb. 28, 1986.\"},{\"text\":\"The Challenger accident killed six astronauts.\",\"line\":9,\"span\":\"The spectacular news of the Uranus encounter was interrupted the same day by the tragic Challenger accident that killed six astronauts during their space shuttle launch Feb. 28, 1986.\"},{\"text\":\"The Challenger accident occurred during the space shuttle launch on Feb. 28, 1986.\",\"line\":9,\"span\":\"The spectacular news of the Uranus encounter was interrupted the same day by the tragic Challenger accident that killed six astronauts during their space shuttle launch Feb. 28, 1986.\"}]}","ttft_ms":30080,"type":"result","duration_ms":30096,"uuid":"319af47a-eff6-475a-b443-64c47b6fc4df","ttft_stream_ms":6523,"time_to_request_ms":4583,"first_content_frame_ms":6524,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
